        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 1089,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> hilus cell tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:19:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1074,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1090,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152630,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 1090,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:19:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1075,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1091,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152631,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 1091,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Leydig cell tumours are centred in and predominantly confined to the ovarian hilum, sometimes with extension into the ovarian parenchyma {2553628}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:19:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1076,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1092,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152632,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 1092,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The usual presentation is with endocrine manifestations, most commonly androgenic and rarely estrogenic {2553628}. In cases where a hormone-producing ovarian neoplasm is suspected clinically but imaging has not been contributory, the location of a Leydig cell tumour may be pinpointed preoperatively by detecting high levels of androgens after selective ovarian vein sampling {33665140; 38045790}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:19:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1077,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1093,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152633,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 1093,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The average patient age in one study was 58&nbsp;years (range: 32&ndash;82&nbsp;years) {2553628}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:19:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1078,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1094,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152634,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 1094,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:19:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1079,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1095,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152635,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 1095,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:19:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1086,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1102,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152636,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 1102,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours are usually small (average size: 20&nbsp;mm) and are rarely bilateral {34137345; 33370966}. The lesions are usually well-circumscribed at the ovarian hilum, and the cut surface is typically solid, lobulated, and soft with a brown, red, or pink appearance {2553628}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:19:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1080,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1096,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152637,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 1096,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours typically comprise a well-circumscribed nodular growth of large polyhedral or rounded epithelioid cells with abundant eosinophilic, but occasionally pale, cytoplasm. Cytoplasmic lipochrome pigment is commonly seen. Clustering of nuclei, creating intervening eosinophilic nuclear-free zones, is a characteristic feature. Cytoplasmic Reinke crystals (rod-shaped elongated eosinophilic crystals) are sometimes seen but may be rare or absent. Nuclei are typically round, with a single prominent nucleolus; nuclear pseudoinclusions may be present, and bizarre nuclear atypia is sometimes seen. Mitoses are rare or absent. In approximately one&nbsp;third of cases, fibrinoid material is present within blood vessel walls. Occasional tumours have a conspicuous fibrous stroma. Hyperplasia of non-neoplastic hilus/Leydig cells is commonly present in the uninvolved ovarian hilum {2553628}. The diagnosis of Leydig cell tumour is appropriate for tumours that lack identifiable Reinke crystals but show otherwise typical features and are located exclusively or predominantly in the ovarian hilum.</p>\r\n<p>Immunohistochemically, Leydig cell tumours are usually positive for sex cord stromal markers, such as inhibin {9688187}, calretinin {11603218}, SF1, and melan-A {20567142}. Cytoplasmic SATB2 immunoreactivity may also occur in Leydig cell tumours {37668387}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:19:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1081,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1097,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152638,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 1097,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:19:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1082,
